1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,393
A lion's body.

2
00:00:03,693 --> 00:00:05,183
A woman's face.

3
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:07,456
Wings folded on her back.

4
00:00:07,756 --> 00:00:12,161
She watches over the city of Thebes,
and asks its people a riddle.

5
00:00:12,461 --> 00:00:15,351
Those who cannot answer, she destroys.

6
00:00:15,651 --> 00:00:18,234
This is the Sphinx of Greek legend.

7
00:00:18,534 --> 00:00:21,281
Yet the most famous sphinx in the world,

8
00:00:21,281 --> 00:00:24,784
the great one in Egypt,
never asked anyone a riddle.

9
00:00:25,084 --> 00:00:27,006
It is not even a woman.

10
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:33,441
In this episode, we look at two very
different sphinxes, the guardian of Egypt

11
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:38,447
and the riddler of Greece, and at how
one name came to cover them both.

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,979
The Great Sphinx of Giza
was carved from the living rock

13
00:00:47,979 --> 00:00:51,658
of the desert, about four
and a half thousand years ago.

14
00:00:51,958 --> 00:00:57,225
Many scholars link it to King Khafre,
whose pyramid rises behind it.

15
00:00:57,525 --> 00:01:00,823
Its face wears the royal
headcloth of a pharaoh.

16
00:01:01,123 --> 00:01:03,608
Its body is a lion at rest.

17
00:01:04,533 --> 00:01:07,978
In Egypt, the lion stood
for power, and the sphinx

18
00:01:07,978 --> 00:01:10,930
joined that power to the face of the king.

19
00:01:11,230 --> 00:01:16,561
Sphinxes lined the roads to temples
and kept watch over sacred places.

20
00:01:16,861 --> 00:01:18,869
Most of them show a king.

21
00:01:19,169 --> 00:01:24,348
Hatshepsut, a woman who ruled as pharaoh,
appears here as a sphinx too,

22
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:28,713
with the royal headcloth
and the ceremonial beard of a king.

23
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:33,494
We do not know what the builders
of Giza called their sphinx.

24
00:01:33,794 --> 00:01:38,012
More than a thousand years
later, Egyptians worshipped

25
00:01:38,012 --> 00:01:42,390
it as a god, Horemakhet,
"Horus in the Horizon." Between

26
00:01:42,390 --> 00:01:47,006
its paws stands a stone set up
by King Thutmose the Fourth.

27
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:52,393
It tells how, as a young prince, he
rested in the shadow of the great god.

28
00:01:52,693 --> 00:01:55,631
In a dream, the god
promised him the kingdom,

29
00:01:55,631 --> 00:01:59,103
and said that the sand
of the desert had reached him.

30
00:02:02,067 --> 00:02:06,065
In these Egyptian statues
and stones, there is no riddle.

31
00:02:06,365 --> 00:02:09,171
The sphinx here does not test travelers.

32
00:02:09,471 --> 00:02:11,552
It guards kings and gods.

33
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,615
In Greece, the sphinx was something else.

34
00:02:16,915 --> 00:02:22,211
The poet Hesiod, writing around
700 BC, lists her among a family

35
00:02:22,211 --> 00:02:27,591
of monsters, and calls her the destroyer
of the people of Thebes.

36
00:02:27,891 --> 00:02:31,198
In this passage, he says
nothing about a riddle.

37
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:35,940
Greek artists usually gave
the sphinx a woman's head,

38
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,819
a lion's body, and the wings of a bird.

39
00:02:39,119 --> 00:02:43,812
Often they set her on top of a grave
marker, watching over the dead.

40
00:02:44,112 --> 00:02:49,560
This sphinx once crowned a grave
in Attica, around 530 BC.

41
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,613
Where the name came from is uncertain.

42
00:02:54,913 --> 00:02:58,542
Some link it to a Greek
word meaning "to squeeze"

43
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:02,700
or "to bind." Others have
looked for an Egyptian origin.

44
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,837
No one knows for sure.

45
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,765
The riddle belongs
to the stories of Thebes,

46
00:03:10,765 --> 00:03:14,247
told on the stage
in Athens in the 400s BC.

47
00:03:14,547 --> 00:03:19,715
In Sophocles' play Oedipus the King,
the people remember "the riddling

48
00:03:19,715 --> 00:03:24,509
Sphinx," and Oedipus is the stranger
who freed the city from her.

49
00:03:24,809 --> 00:03:28,156
Painters have returned
to this meeting ever since.

50
00:03:31,667 --> 00:03:34,623
But the play never gives
the words of the riddle.

51
00:03:34,923 --> 00:03:38,421
We find them in later
writers, such as Athenaeus,

52
00:03:38,421 --> 00:03:41,481
and a handbook of myths
called the Library.

53
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:47,480
They ask: what has one voice, and becomes
four-footed, two-footed, and three-footed?

54
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:50,129
The answer is a human being.

55
00:03:50,429 --> 00:03:54,682
A baby crawls, an adult walks,
and the old lean on a staff.

56
00:03:54,982 --> 00:03:58,981
In these texts, there is no
morning, noon, or evening.

57
00:03:59,281 --> 00:04:01,353
And when the riddle was solved,

58
00:04:01,353 --> 00:04:04,494
the Library says, the Sphinx
threw herself down.

59
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,813
So how did one name come to mean both?

60
00:04:13,113 --> 00:04:15,043
We do not know every step.

61
00:04:15,343 --> 00:04:17,084
But the Greek name stayed.

62
00:04:17,384 --> 00:04:21,798
When the historian Herodotus,
in the 400s BC, described

63
00:04:21,798 --> 00:04:26,783
the great statues a king set up
at a temple in Sais, in Egypt,

64
00:04:26,783 --> 00:04:31,687
he called them androsphinxes,
sphinxes with the heads of men.

65
00:04:31,987 --> 00:04:36,703
And we still call the guardian of Giza
by the name of a Greek monster.

66
00:04:38,833 --> 00:04:41,119
Its face carries another story.

67
00:04:41,419 --> 00:04:45,971
It is often said that Napoleon's
soldiers shot off its nose.

68
00:04:46,271 --> 00:04:50,210
Yet drawings by the Danish
traveler Frederic Norden,

69
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:55,153
who visited in the 1730s, already
show the face without its nose.

70
00:04:55,453 --> 00:05:01,892
They were printed long before
Napoleon came to Egypt in 1798.

71
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:05,075
So, did the Sphinx ask riddles?

72
00:05:05,375 --> 00:05:08,447
The Greek one did,
in the stories of Thebes.

73
00:05:08,747 --> 00:05:12,394
The Egyptian one, far older,
was a king in the shape

74
00:05:12,394 --> 00:05:15,969
of a lion, keeping watch
at the edge of the desert.

75
00:05:16,269 --> 00:05:18,222
Two creatures, one name.

76
00:05:18,522 --> 00:05:20,839
Every story has an origin.

77
00:05:21,139 --> 00:05:24,307
Sometimes, two stories
share the same name.

78
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:33,993
This video is based on historical
texts and public museum collections.

79
00:05:34,293 --> 00:05:37,249
The narration uses synthetic speech,

80
00:05:37,249 --> 00:05:41,191
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

81
00:05:41,491 --> 00:05:45,447
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

82
00:05:45,747 --> 00:05:48,516
If you enjoyed this story, please like

83
00:05:48,516 --> 00:05:51,868
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

84
00:05:52,168 --> 00:05:53,941
Thank you for watching.
